string submitCustomerLocation(string recordId, string locationType, string lat, string lon)
{
	returnstr = "";
	try
	{
		result = Map();
		// ================= CREATOR DATA API (OAuth) =================
		// 2026-09-16. The direct Deluge write (rec.Latitude = ...) is REPLACED by
		// an authenticated PATCH to the Creator Data API, because the direct
		// write is impossible to make work here.
		//
		// PROVEN by this function's own logging, running the identical script
		// against the identical record: from the Execute dialog the read probe
		// passes AND the write succeeds; over the public URL the read probe
		// passes and the write fails with "'rec' has no matching records". Read
		// allowed, write refused - i.e. the user a public invocation runs as has
		// View but not Edit on Create_Case. No change to the Deluge write itself
		// can fix that; it is a permission, not a syntax.
		//
		// The Data API carries its OWN authorization via this OAuth token rather
		// than inheriting the caller's, which is exactly how onDemandInvoicing/
		// recordVendorPayment already write to Zoho Books from a public context.
		// Same principle, Creator instead of Books.
		//
		// Credentials user-provided 2026-09-16, scope ZohoCreator.report.ALL.
		// client_id/client_secret are the same OAuth app already used for Zoho
		// Payments in onDemandInvoicing.deluge; the REFRESH TOKEN is a different
		// one, issued for the Creator scope.
		ZC_CLIENT_ID = "1000.7BYOUWK2Q2XMJQWU62E6WHCRZOY9VW";
		ZC_CLIENT_SECRET = "a714fc6ab6c76d058196ceef8b49cad735422987fd";
		ZC_REFRESH_TOKEN = "1000.568bbdff83b5b398edb888ff31951dea.a5cbb7bbdd283c79ec801ebd10be426a";
		// Report, not form - the Data API addresses records through a report.
		// Agent_Ticket_Report is the same report the whole app already reads and
		// updates Create_Case through (see getRescueTicket CONFIG.reportName).
		ZC_RECORD_URL_BASE = "https://www.zohoapis.in/creator/v2.1/data/getrescued/get-rescue/report/Agent_Ticket_Report/";
		// ENTRY LOG (2026-09-16, user request) - every parameter exactly as it
		// arrived, before anything touches it. Values are wrapped in [square
		// brackets] on purpose: that is the only way to see leading/trailing
		// whitespace, an empty string, or a stray punctuation character at a
		// glance - all three have already caused real bugs in this one function
		// (a trailing space that crashed .toLong(), and a trailing "." on
		// locationType). "(null)" means the parameter was not bound at all,
		// which for a Custom API usually means it is not registered in the
		// API's own setup screen.
		info "========== submitCustomerLocation CALLED ==========";
		info "  recordId     = [" + ifnull(recordId,"(null)") + "]";
		info "  locationType = [" + ifnull(locationType,"(null)") + "]";
		info "  lat          = [" + ifnull(lat,"(null)") + "]";
		info "  lon          = [" + ifnull(lon,"(null)") + "]";
		// .trim() added 2026-09-10 - same real, confirmed-live bug already
		// found and fixed in generateZohoPaymentLink.deluge the same day
		// (a stray trailing space in recordId crashed .toLong() outright
		// there); added here defensively too, zero risk either way.
		// 2026-09-16 - the record ID is now resolved into its OWN variable before
		// the criteria, instead of calling .trim().toLong() inline inside it.
		//
		// This mirrors paymentWebhook.deluge exactly, which is the ONE script in
		// this project CONFIRMED to write to Create_Case over a public URL (it
		// marks Booking Fee payments PAID, user-verified live). It resolves its
		// id first - resolvedRecordId = ...toLong(); then Create_Case[ID ==
		// resolvedRecordId] - and succeeds. This function did the conversion
		// inline in the criteria and has failed over a public URL every time,
		// with both a single fetch and a double fetch, so the fetch count is
		// ruled out and the inline expression is what is actually left.
		//
		// generateZohoPaymentLink.deluge uses the inline form and works, but it
		// is invoked through invokeCustomApi as a logged-in agent, not over a
		// public URL - a different execution context entirely, which is why it
		// is the weaker model to copy here and paymentWebhook is the better one.
		targetRecordId = recordId.trim().toLong();
		info "  targetRecordId (after trim + toLong) = " + targetRecordId;
		rec = Create_Case[ID == targetRecordId];
		// READABILITY PROBE (2026-09-14) - the key insight into why this call
		// has worked from the Execute dialog for days while failing over the
		// public URL with "'recForWrite' has no matching records".
		//
		// `rec == null` below is NOT a reliable existence test. When a Deluge
		// record query matches nothing - including when it matches nothing
		// because the EXECUTING USER cannot see the record - it does not
		// necessarily come back as null. The null check therefore passes, the
		// function runs all the way down to the write, and only THERE does it
		// fail, with a "has no matching records" error that names the write
		// variable and so looks like a problem with the write. It never was:
		// the fetch found nothing in the first place.
		//
		// That fits every symptom exactly. The Execute dialog runs as the
		// developer, who can see every record, so it succeeds. The public URL
		// runs as whatever user this Custom API is configured to execute as; if
		// that user has no permission on Create_Case, the query returns nothing
		// and the write blows up 130 lines later with a misleading message.
		//
		// This probe reads one harmless field immediately after the fetch, in
		// its own try/catch. If that throws, the record is not readable HERE and
		// now, and the caller gets told precisely that instead of a cryptic
		// failure at write time. It changes no behaviour for a working call.
		recReadable = true;
		try
		{
			probeText = ifnull(rec.Case_ID,"").toString();
			info "  READ PROBE OK - rec.Case_ID = [" + probeText + "] (the record IS readable in this execution context)";
		}
		catch (probeEx)
		{
			recReadable = false;
			info "submitCustomerLocation - record " + recordId + " matched no readable row. This is almost always a PERMISSION issue, not a data one: compare this Custom API's own 'Execute the script as' setting against paymentWebhook's, which does succeed over a public URL. Raw error: " + probeEx.toString();
		}
		if(rec == null)
		{
			result.put("code", "ERROR");
			result.put("message", "Ticket not found");
			returnstr = result.toString();
		}
		else if(recReadable == false)
		{
			result.put("code", "ERROR");
			result.put("message", "Ticket " + recordId + " could not be read by this Custom API. The ticket most likely exists - check this Custom API's 'Execute the script as' user and its permission on the Create_Case form. Nothing was written.");
			returnstr = result.toString();
		}
		else if(lat == null || lat == "" || lon == null || lon == "")
		{
			result.put("code", "ERROR");
			result.put("message", "Latitude/Longitude missing");
			returnstr = result.toString();
		}
		else
		{
			// Validate + normalize (2026-09-10, explicit user request:
			// "Validate the coordinates and handle both map-selected and
			// manually entered latitude/longitude values correctly") - lat/lon
			// arrive as raw strings from either the customer's map pin (its own
			// floating-point pan/zoom math) or a device GPS fix, both of which
			// can carry 15-17+ decimal digits. .toDecimal()/.round(6) (both
			// confirmed working Deluge syntax already used the same way
			// elsewhere in this project - see recordVendorPayment.deluge's own
			// ".toDecimal()"/".round(2)") caps that at ~11cm of real-world
			// precision, well under the real (unconfirmed exact value) "Decimal
			// field exceeded maximum digits" limit getRescueTicket's own
			// roundCoord()/saveWithCoordFallback() were built to work around on
			// these SAME Latitude/Longitude/DropLocationLat/DropLocationLong
			// fields - this call had no protection against that error at all
			// before now. Range-checked against real GPS bounds so a malformed
			// value (or a future caller hitting this Custom API directly)
			// never silently writes garbage into the ticket.
			latVal = lat.toDecimal().round(6);
			lonVal = lon.toDecimal().round(6);
			// locationType normalization (2026-09-10, widened after a live
			// "Unknown locationType: breakdown." failure - a stray trailing
			// period arrived from wherever this particular link/navigation was
			// generated, outside this repo's own confirmed-clean URL-building
			// code - see getRescueTicket/app/widget.html's buildCustomerPageUrl(),
			// checked and confirmed to build a plain "breakdown"/"drop" with no
			// punctuation). Rather than keep chasing one hardcoded punctuation
			// variant at a time (the previous fix only handled an exact
			// trailing "."), this matches by SUBSTRING on a trimmed, lowercased
			// value instead - covers "breakdown", "breakdown.", "Breakdown ",
			// "BREAKDOWN..", stray whitespace, etc. for BOTH Breakdown and Drop
			// symmetrically, not just the one case actually seen live so far.
			// .trim()/.toLowerCase()/.contains() are all already confirmed
			// working elsewhere in this project (see createServiceRequest.deluge's
			// own "issueTypeText.toLowerCase().contains("tow")") - no new/
			// unverified Deluge syntax introduced (the previous fix's brace-less
			// "if(...) x = y;" lines were a real, confirmed-live "Improper
			// Statement" error; every branch below is fully braced). Only
			// "breakdown"/"drop" are ever real values here, so a substring match
			// can't collide with anything else.
			locTypeNorm = ifnull(locationType,"").trim().toLowerCase();
			isDrop = locTypeNorm.contains("drop");
			isBreakdown = locTypeNorm.contains("breakdown");
			if(latVal < -90 || latVal > 90 || lonVal < -180 || lonVal > 180)
			{
				result.put("code", "ERROR");
				result.put("message", "Invalid coordinates");
				returnstr = result.toString();
			}
			else if(isDrop || isBreakdown)
			{
				// Real root cause CONFIRMED 2026-09-10 via a Zoho Studio
				// screenshot: Latitude/Longitude/DropLocationLat/DropLocationLong
				// were Decimal fields with Max Digits=16, Decimal Points=15.
				// Zoho's Decimal type works like SQL DECIMAL(precision,scale) -
				// the WHOLE-NUMBER part can only ever hold (Max Digits - Decimal
				// Points) digits. Every real-world latitude/longitude has a
				// 2-3 digit whole-number part (e.g. "28" in 28.613977, "77" in
				// 77.209023), so at Max Digits=16/Decimal Points=15 every
				// possible coordinate was mathematically too large for the
				// field's configuration at the time - no rounding or retry
				// could ever have worked around it.
				//
				// RESOLVED 2026-09-11 (user-confirmed): rather than keep
				// widening the Decimal config, all 4 of these fields (plus
				// RSP_Start_Latitude/Longitude, RSP_Drop_Latitude/Longitude,
				// and Cancel_Location_Lat/Lon elsewhere in this app) were
				// converted to Single Line Text instead - no digit limit at
				// all, so this entire error class can no longer fire on any
				// coordinate field in this app. The write below (a Deluge
				// string assigned to rec.Latitude/etc.) needed no code change
				// for this - it already wrote a string either way, which
				// Deluge auto-converts on assignment to a Decimal field, and
				// which a Text field accepts even more directly.
				//
				// An earlier version of this function retried the write at
				// 6/4/2/0 decimal places, hoping fewer decimals would fit -
				// that hypothesis was DISPROVEN by the Decimal-precision math
				// above: reducing decimal places never changes the whole-
				// number part, so all 4 of those attempts were guaranteed to
				// fail identically, every time. Removed as dead code even
				// before the Text conversion made it doubly unnecessary. The
				// returned error message (visible only in this Custom API's
				// own execution log and the browser console -
				// customerPage/app/widget.html's submitLocation() never shows
				// this raw message to the customer, only a generic "please try
				// again" - see its own renderError() call) now just surfaces
				// whatever Zoho actually says, since the old digit-limit cause
				// no longer applies.
				try
				{
					latToWrite = latVal.toString();
					lonToWrite = lonVal.toString();
					// PRE-WRITE LOG - the exact values and the exact fields about
					// to be written. If the next thing in the log is the write
					// failure, this line is the last known-good state and says
					// precisely what was attempted.
					if(isDrop)
					{
						info "  ABOUT TO WRITE (drop): DropLocationLat=[" + latToWrite + "] DropLocationLong=[" + lonToWrite + "] on record " + targetRecordId;
					}
					else
					{
						info "  ABOUT TO WRITE (breakdown): Latitude=[" + latToWrite + "] Longitude=[" + lonToWrite + "] on record " + targetRecordId;
					}
					// 2026-09-13 - REVERTED the previous attempt here (a bulk
					// `update Create_Case set ... where ...;` statement),
					// confirmed live in Zoho's own script editor to be an
					// "Improper Statement" - that exact syntax is not valid in
					// this account/version, so it's ruled out cleanly, with no
					// live/customer-facing impact (it never got past Save).
					// Back to the dot-notation write, independently proven
					// working elsewhere in this project against this same
					// Create_Case form (generateZohoPaymentLink.deluge's own
					// `rec.Zoho_Payment_URL = ...`) - the one thing added this
					// time is a FRESH re-fetch of the record immediately before
					// the write (recForWrite, right below), using the exact
					// same already-proven `Create_Case[ID == ...]` pattern as
					// the original fetch at the top of this function - a
					// zero-new-syntax hedge against the original runtime error
					// ("'rec' has no matching records. Unable to update the
					// value rec.Latitude.") being caused by the much-earlier
					// `rec` reference having gone stale by the time this
					// point in the function runs, rather than the write
					// pattern itself. Not confirmed to fix the underlying
					// issue - if this exact error recurs even with a fresh
					// fetch right here, that rules out staleness and points
					// squarely at something Zoho-side (this Custom API's own
					// "Execute the script as" permission, compared against
					// generateZohoPaymentLink's - check that next, directly in
					// Zoho, not in code).
					// 2026-09-14 - the `recForWrite` SECOND FETCH that used to sit
					// here is REMOVED. It was added 2026-09-13 as a hedge against
					// `rec` having gone stale; that hypothesis is now disproven.
					// generateZohoPaymentLink.deluge hit this exact error for the
					// opposite reason, and its own comment records the finding:
					// every write in this project that WORKS fetches the record
					// once and writes onto it, and every one that failed this way
					// fetched the same record twice. The writes below now go to
					// `rec` - fetched at the top, and just proven readable by the
					// probe - which is the pattern with a live fix behind it.
					// STEP 1 - exchange the refresh token for an access token.
					// Only grant_type + the three credentials are sent: scope and
					// redirect_uri are meaningful on the ORIGINAL authorization_code
					// exchange that issued the refresh token, not on a refresh
					// grant. Same call shape onDemandInvoicing.deluge already uses
					// successfully for Zoho Payments.
					tokenResp = invokeurl
					[
						url :"https://accounts.zoho.in/oauth/v2/token?refresh_token=" + ZC_REFRESH_TOKEN + "&client_id=" + ZC_CLIENT_ID + "&client_secret=" + ZC_CLIENT_SECRET + "&grant_type=refresh_token"
						type :POST
					];
					zcAccessToken = tokenResp.get("access_token");
					if(zcAccessToken == null)
					{
						info "  TOKEN FAILED - no access_token came back. Raw response: " + tokenResp.toString();
						result.put("code", "ERROR");
						result.put("message", "Could not authorise with Zoho Creator (no access token). Nothing was written - see this Custom API's execution log.");
						return result.toString();
					}
					info "  TOKEN OK - access token obtained";

					// STEP 2 - PATCH the coordinate fields. The Data API answers
					// with code 3000 on success, the same convention the widgets'
					// own getRecords calls already check for.
					coordMap = Map();
					if(isDrop)
					{
						coordMap.put("DropLocationLat",latToWrite);
						coordMap.put("DropLocationLong",lonToWrite);
					}
					else
					{
						coordMap.put("Latitude",latToWrite);
						coordMap.put("Longitude",lonToWrite);
					}
					coordBody = Map();
					coordBody.put("data",coordMap);
					patchResp = invokeurl
					[
						url :ZC_RECORD_URL_BASE + targetRecordId
						type :PATCH
						parameters:coordBody.toString()
						headers:{"Content-Type":"application/json","Authorization":"Bearer " + zcAccessToken}
					];
					info "  PATCH response: " + patchResp.toString();
					patchCode = patchResp.get("code");
					if(patchCode != 3000)
					{
						patchMsg = patchResp.get("message");
						info "  PATCH FAILED - code " + patchCode + " message " + patchMsg;
						result.put("code", "ERROR");
						result.put("message", "Could not save this coordinate - Zoho Creator API said: " + patchMsg);
						return result.toString();
					}
					// Drop_Location_Received_Time / Location_Received_Time
					// (2026-09-11, part of the wider "capture location+time at
					// every relevant step" request) - user has now created
					// Drop_Location_Received_Time in Zoho (Location_Received_Time
					// already existed, but was previously only ever stamped
					// later, whenever the agent next happened to revisit the
					// Locations wizard step - see getRescueTicket/app/widget.html's
					// own "locations" stage extra(), which still runs too and is
					// left completely unchanged as a harmless no-op once this
					// field is already set - see that file's own matching
					// comment). Stamping it HERE instead means it now reflects
					// the ACTUAL moment the customer's coordinate was saved, not
					// whenever an agent's screen happened to catch up.
					// zoho.currenttime is Deluge's own documented "current date
					// + time" system variable (confirmed via Zoho's own Deluge
					// System Variables reference) - assigned directly to a
					// native Date-Time field, no string formatting. Wrapped in
					// its OWN try/catch, separate from the lat/lon write just
					// above: this is a genuinely new write in a genuinely new
					// context (a Custom API record UPDATE, not the simpler
					// form-submit-insert context most public examples show) and
					// hasn't been independently confirmed live yet - if the
					// exact value/format Deluge produces here turns out not to
					// be accepted by a Date-Time field in an update context,
					// this must NEVER be allowed to take down the actual
					// coordinate save above it, which is this function's one
					// truly critical job and was already working correctly
					// before this change. Reuses the same recForWrite reference
					// as the lat/lon write just above (already confirmed live by
					// this point), not a second fresh fetch.
					// Sent as its OWN SECOND PATCH, deliberately not bundled with the
					// coordinates above. A Date-Time field's accepted format over
					// the Data API has never been confirmed in this project; if it
					// is wrong, one combined PATCH would be rejected in full and
					// take the coordinates down with it. Separate calls mean the
					// critical write has already committed before this is even
					// attempted - the same "must never block the coordinate save"
					// contract this block has always had, now enforced structurally
					// rather than just by a try/catch.
					try
					{
						tsMap = Map();
						if(isDrop)
						{
							tsMap.put("Drop_Location_Received_Time",zoho.currenttime.toString("dd-MMM-yyyy HH:mm:ss"));
						}
						else
						{
							tsMap.put("Location_Received_Time",zoho.currenttime.toString("dd-MMM-yyyy HH:mm:ss"));
						}
						tsBody = Map();
						tsBody.put("data",tsMap);
						tsResp = invokeurl
						[
							url :ZC_RECORD_URL_BASE + targetRecordId
							type :PATCH
							parameters:tsBody.toString()
							headers:{"Content-Type":"application/json","Authorization":"Bearer " + zcAccessToken}
						];
						info "  timestamp PATCH response: " + tsResp.toString();
					}
					catch (tsEx)
					{
						info "submitCustomerLocation - timestamp write failed (non-blocking, the coordinate itself was still saved successfully): " + tsEx.toString();
					}
					info "  WRITE OK - coordinate saved on record " + targetRecordId;
					result.put("code", "SUCCESS");
					returnstr = result.toString();
				}
				catch (writeEx)
				{
					info "  WRITE FAILED on record " + targetRecordId + " - Zoho said: " + writeEx.toString();
					result.put("code", "ERROR");
					// Message updated 2026-09-11 (later) - Latitude/Longitude/
					// DropLocationLat/DropLocationLong were converted from Decimal
					// to Single Line Text this same day (user-confirmed), so the
					// digit-limit cause this message used to describe no longer
					// applies at all - a stale explanation here would now be
					// actively misleading. If this branch fires, it's genuinely a
					// different cause; just surface Zoho's own error text and let
					// whoever's debugging look at the execution log directly.
					result.put("message", "Could not save this coordinate - Zoho error: " + writeEx.toString());
					returnstr = result.toString();
				}
			}
			else
			{
				result.put("code", "ERROR");
				result.put("message", "Unknown locationType: " + locationType);
				returnstr = result.toString();
			}
		}
	}
	catch (e)
	{
		errResult = Map();
		errResult.put("code", "ERROR");
		errResult.put("message", e.toString());
		returnstr = errResult.toString();
	}
	return returnstr;
}
